<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel da Nóbrega</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1517">1517</a>–<a href="/years/1570">1570</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel da Nóbrega was a Portuguese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesuits">Jesuit</a> who led the first Jesuit mission to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, arriving with the governor-general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tome-de-sousa">Tomé de Sousa</a> in 1549. He organised missions among the indigenous peoples, opposed their enslavement and in 1554, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-de-anchieta">José de Anchieta</a>, founded the college from which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sao-paulo">São Paulo</a> grew. He also helped negotiate peace with the Tamoio in 1563.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-da-nobrega.jpg" alt="Manuel da Nóbrega" width="160" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nobrega2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1549 the king made colonisation a royal enterprise, sending Tomé de Sousa as first governor-general with the first Jesuits under <b>Manuel da Nóbrega</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/colonial-brazil">Colonial Brazil · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Manuel da Nóbrega</b> and José de Anchieta celebrated Mass at a new Jesuit mission on the Piratininga plateau.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-25">25 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The captaincy grew from sugar mills on the coast, but its future lay on the plateau, where the Jesuits <b>Manuel da Nóbrega</b> and José de Anchieta founded São Paulo in 1554.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-of-sao-vicente">Captaincy of São Vicente</a></li><li><blockquote>Tomé de Sousa arrived as the first governor-general with soldiers, officials and the first Jesuits under <b>Manuel da Nóbrega</b>, and founded Salvador on the Bay of All Saints as capital.</blockquote><a href="/state/state-of-brazil">State of Brazil</a></li></ul></section>
